{"pdfpcFormat":2,"disableMarkdown":true,"noteFontSize":20,"pages":[{"idx":0,"label":"1","overlay":0,"note":"Today, I will present how my research direction evolved this week. I reviewed multi-agent failure studies and LLM swarm benchmarks. I also examined Bolzano, an AI-assisted mathematical research system, to understand the value of parallel exploration and shared knowledge.\n"},{"idx":1,"label":"2","overlay":0,"note":"Last week, I studied reliable consensus among LLM agents. My direction was to combine deterministic verification with fault-tolerant agreement. However, I had not identified a concrete application that truly needed distributed agreement.\n"},{"idx":2,"label":"3","overlay":0,"note":"The main feedback was to clarify the motivation and system model before proposing a consensus protocol. I need to define the target layer, correctness, and fault assumptions. I also need to show why existing mechanisms are insufficient.\n"},{"idx":3,"label":"4","overlay":0,"note":"I reconsidered agreement as the primary goal. For example, coordinating agents in one browser can sometimes be handled by ordinary mutual exclusion. This week, I shifted toward tasks where agents cooperate to achieve an outcome that we can verify.\n"},{"idx":4,"label":"5","overlay":0,"note":"An inspiration came from recent AI-assisted mathematics research. Parallel agents could explore different ideas and share useful results. But this benefit should not be assumed. We must compare cooperation with independent parallel search using the same compute budget.\n"},{"idx":5,"label":"6","overlay":0,"note":"I examined four lines of related work. The first studies failures, the second fault resilience, and the third local swarm coordination. Bolzano offers an example of difficult tasks involving exploration, verification, and shared research state. Together, they help narrow the application and system model.\n"},{"idx":6,"label":"7","overlay":0,"note":"SwarmBench evaluates agents with local observations and nearby communication. Its tasks have measurable outcomes, such as pursuit or cooperative transport. The point is that agents must coordinate under limited information. This is an existing benchmark, not my own proposed model.\n"},{"idx":7,"label":"8","overlay":0,"note":"Bolzano separates mathematical proof exploration from checking and summarization. Its architecture can use multiple provers, while a verifier controls updates to shared research state. Importantly, the later large-scale experiment used one prover per problem, so its reported successes do not directly establish a parallel-agent advantage.\n"},{"idx":8,"label":"9","overlay":0,"note":"Bolzano reports meaningful mathematical research outcomes. However, those outcomes do not isolate the effect of using multiple provers. We need controlled comparisons between single-agent search, independent parallel agents, and agents that share verified information. This distinction is essential for motivation.\n"},{"idx":9,"label":"10","overlay":0,"note":"This is a possible comparison for future work, not an experiment I have conducted. The key baseline is independent parallel search. If communication does not outperform that baseline under equal compute budgets, there may be little reason to introduce distributed coordination.\n"},{"idx":10,"label":"11","overlay":0,"note":"One idea is to study when intermediate discoveries should be shared. Early sharing may avoid duplicate work, but it can spread incorrect claims. Waiting for verification may be safer but slower. This could connect to distributed information propagation and consistency, although novelty over existing systems research is not established.\n"},{"idx":11,"label":"12","overlay":0,"note":"Next week, I want to select one verifiable task, such as programming or formal proof search. I will examine whether sharing intermediate findings improves performance over independent search. I also need to identify a genuine distributed-systems problem rather than simply applying existing coordination tools.\n"},{"idx":12,"label":"13","overlay":0,"note":"These are the main papers behind this week's literature review. No new algorithm or experimental result is claimed. Thank you for listening."}]}